%I A399521 #32 Nov 10 2026 14:05:04
%S A399521 1,2,4,4,8,8,8,8,16,16,16,16,16,16,16,16,32,32,32,32,32,32,32,32,32,
%T A399521 32,32,32,32,32,32,32,64,64,64,64,64,64,64,64,64,64,64,64,64,64,64,64,
%U A399521 64,128,128,128,256,256,256,512,512,512,512,512,512,512,512
%N A399521 Least eventual period of row n of the array T(x,y) = mex{c: T(x-d,y) = T(x,y-d) = T(x-d,y-d) = c for some 1 <= d <= min(x,y)}, x,y >= 0.
%C A399521 T(x,y) is the least nonnegative color c such that no square with corners (x,y), (x-d,y), (x,y-d), (x-d,y-d), 1 <= d <= min(x,y), has all four corners colored c. Equivalently, T read by antidiagonals is the lexicographically earliest array of nonnegative integers with no axis-parallel square whose four corners are equal (A401735). Row n is x -> T(x,n).
%C A399521 a(n) is the least p >= 1 such that T(x+p,n) = T(x,n) for all sufficiently large x.
%C A399521 T is symmetric and 0 <= T(x,y) <= min(x,y), since each smaller color needs its own side d. Every row is eventually periodic: the periodic phases of the lower rows together with the preceding n entries of row n form a finite deterministic state. This gives the bound in the formula section.
%C A399521 T(x,y) = 0 if and only if x AND y = 0 (bitwise AND, A004198), i.e., if and only if binomial(x+y,x) is odd; so the color-0 cells form the Sierpinski triangle A047999 rotated by 45 degrees. They are also the greedy squarefree set: a cell gets color 0 unless it completes a square whose other three corners have color 0. That the greedy algorithm gives this rotated triangle was observed by N. J. A. Sloane in his comment of Jan 18 2016 in A047999.
%C A399521 Proof of the zero set (induction on x+y): if e = x AND y > 0, the other three corners (x-e,y), (x,y-e), (x-e,y-e) of the square of side e have AND 0, hence color 0, so T(x,y) > 0. If x AND y = 0, then for every side d one of the other three corners has bit v2(d) (the 2-adic valuation of d, A007814) set in both coordinates, hence a nonzero color, so T(x,y) = 0.
%C A399521 Consequently the zero set of row n is purely periodic with least period A062383(n) = 2^bitlength(n) (period word A268229(n)), which divides a(n); bitlength(0) = 0.
%C A399521 Every nonnegative integer occurs in T: by the Gallai-Witt theorem, for each k every large enough square block contains a color >= k, and a cell of color c >= 1 at (x,y) has a cell of color c-1 at some (x-d,y-d). Applying the theorem to a block beyond the preperiods of all rows meeting it shows that the maxima of the periodic parts of the rows are also unbounded.
%C A399521 Certification lemma: if rows 0,...,n-1 are M-periodic from column u and T(x+M,n) = T(x,n) for u <= x < u+n, then row n is M-periodic from u (induction on x: for x >= u+n the definition reads row n only on [x-n,x) and rows < n only on [x-n,x]). The least period of row n is then the least divisor q of M with T(x+q,n) = T(x,n) for u <= x < u+M.
%C A399521 Every term n <= 211 was certified by a program using this lemma and finite minimality tests. A second, independently written program recomputed rows 0..203 and verified each certificate; for n <= 202 it also found a(n) without the certificates, by exact cycle detection on the joint state of rows 0..n. Terms 204..211 rest on the first program only.
%C A399521 Computation gives a(n) = A062383(n) for 0 <= n <= 48, with first failure a(49) = 128. Among 0 <= n <= 211, equality holds precisely for n = 0..48 and n = 64.
%C A399521 a(n) = A296613(n+1) for 0 <= n <= 48, but a(49) = 128 while A296613(50) = 64. Also a(79) = 6144 = 2^11*3, whereas no term of A296613 has a prime factor other than 2 and 5.
%C A399521 For all 0 <= n <= 210 except n = 63, computation gives a(n) | a(n+1). At the exception, a(63) = 2048 and a(64) = 128; a(65) = 2048. Conjecture: a(n) | a(n+1) for every n >= 64.
%C A399521 The first occurrences of factors 3, 5, 5^2 and 3^2 are at n = 79, 94, 131 and 203, respectively: a(79) = 6144, a(94) = 30720, a(131) = 1228800, a(203) = 15099494400. The only prime factors occurring for 0 <= n <= 211 are 2, 3 and 5.
%C A399521 For n = 2^k, each block [2bn,2(b+1)n), b >= 0, of row n starts with n zeros, and its second half is a function of the lower rows on that second half alone: a square reaching into the first half has a corner of color 0 there, so it can only exclude color 0, which is excluded anyway since x AND n != 0. Hence a(n) divides lcm(2n, a(0), ..., a(n-1)).
%D A399521 R. L. Graham, B. L. Rothschild and J. H. Spencer, Ramsey Theory, 2nd ed., Wiley, New York, 1990.
%H A399521 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A399521/b399521.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..211</a>
